# Melt-Quenched Hybrid Glasses from Metal-Organic Frameworks

## abstract
While glasses formed by quenching the molten states of inorganic non-metallic, organic, and metallic species are known, those containing both inorganic and organic moieties are far less prevalent. Network materials consisting of inorganic nodes linked by organic ligands do however exist in the crystalline or amorphous domain. This large family of open framework compounds, called metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) or coordination polymers, has been investigated intensively in the past two decades for a variety of applications, almost all of which stem from their high internal surface areas and chemical versatility. Recently, a selection of MOFs has been demonstrated to undergo melting and vitrification upon cooling. Here, these recent discoveries and the connections between the fields of MOF chemistry and glass science are summarized. Possible advantages and applications for MOF glasses produced by utilizing the tunable chemistry of the crystalline state are also highlighted.

While glasses formed by quenching the molten states of inorganic nonmetallic, organic, and metallic species are known, those containing both inorganic and organic moieties are far less prevalent. Network materials consisting of inorganic nodes linked by organic ligands do however exist in the crystalline or amorphous domain. This large family of open framework compounds, called metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) or coordination polymers, has been investigated intensively in the past two decades for a variety of applications, almost all of which stem from their high internal surface areas and chemical versatility. Recently, a selection of MOFs has been dem­onstrated to undergo melting and vitrification upon cooling. Here, these recent discoveries and the connections between the fields of MOF chemistry and glass science are summarized. Possible advantages and applications for MOF glasses produced by utilizing the tunable chemistry of the crystalline state are also highlighted.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), coordination polymers, soft porous crystals, and metal–organic coordination networks[1–4] are of current interest due to their similarity to widely used, conventional porous materials such as zeolites. Crucially, however, MOFs offer a greater potential for future utilization due to their modifiable organic and inorganic building units.[5] Substitution or functionalization of these constituent building blocks has been suggested as a route to target or tune specific properties, and thus lead to a variety of interesting optical, electrical, and magnetic behaviors. Using these proposed rational crystal-engineering strategies, stimuli-responsive materials have been synthesized for use in, e.g., gas separation and storage,[6,7] catalysis,[8] purification,[9] chemical sensing,[10] proton conduction,[11] biomedical imaging, and drug delivery.[12]
Alongside the development of cost effective, green synthetic methods[13,14] and resolution of complicated defect structures,[15] the poor physical and mechanical properties of crystalline MOFs[16] are arguably the biggest impediments to their widespread application. Their collapse under pressure[17] and shear stress[18] has been observed to proceed more quickly for those high-surface-area materials, which is of concern given the current emphasis on producing materials of ultrahigh porosity.[19] Such factors, along with the near-ubiquitous irreversible thermal decomposition (i.e., pyrolysis of the organic ligands and associated destruction of covalent bonds) of MOFs upon heating to moderate (by comparison with oxides and more conventional materials) temperatures, limit their processability or shaping into films, membranes, or fibers. To surmount these drawbacks, an ideal solution would be to find a MOF structure that can be melted before decomposition, enabling casting or shaping before vitrification of the melt upon quenching.
Recently, an international research team succeeded in melting four MOF structures prior to decomposition, and obtained a glass by quenching.[20–22] The liquids themselves are also of interest, although little characterization had been published at the time of writing. This discovery, which linked the melting and vitrification process to chemical and topological conditions,[22] is also linked to the simultaneous research on the melting and vitrification of zinc phosphate–azole coordination polymers with lower dimensionalities.[23–25] Such research opens a new field, in which we might gain a deeper understanding of the nature of glass formation and the glass transition, which is regarded as one of the most interesting and complicated problems in condensed matter science.[26]
Melt-quenched glasses, in the chemical sense, used to be divided into three categories: inorganic non-metallic (e.g. oxide glasses, chalcogenide glasses), organic (polymer), and metallic. Whereas MOFs are not yet at the large-scale commercialization stage, major applications of inorganic non-metallic and polymer glasses in photonics (e.g., telecommunications) are already established. In particular, their unique viscosity–temperature relation ensures relatively good processabilities and that they can be easily shaped into pellet, fiber, film, and various desired forms.
Bulk metallic glasses, on the other hand, have a unique array of mechanical properties (ultrahigh yield strengths, large elastic strain limits, high hardness, corrosion resistance) and the ability to be processed in a manner analogous to plastics, which has led to extensive studies in their use as potential structural engineering materials.[27] Recently, hybrid composites[28] or sol-gel materials have emerged as candidates in which to integrate the low-density, high toughness, and good plasticity of polymers; the high environmental sensitivity of biological materials;[29] and the large hardness, high mechanical strength, and long term stability of inorganic materials.
The recent synthesis of several melt-quenched glasses from MOFs and coordination polymers with lower dimensionality provides a link between hybrid coordination chemistry and the glass formation. In this research news, we describe the interdisciplinary scientific significance of these recent discoveries, highlighting potential technical applications and predicting possible future directions for the non-crystalline hybrid materials domain.
## 2. Distinction and Prevalence
While the dominant route for glass formation is the quenching of a supercooled melt below the melting point $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ ,[30,31] other routes such as crystal compression,[32] solvent evaporation,[33] vapor deposition,[34–37] and sol-gel condensation[38] are also effective. Notably, mechanical milling has been used to induce amorphization of the coordination polymer crystals,[16,39] although the mechanically amorphized materials are rather different from the melt-quenched glasses in terms of potential energy. The melt-quenched glasses are arguably more promising due to their ability to be physically shaped. Some MOF structures were observed to collapse to an amorphous phase (non-glassy state) upon post-synthetic removal of pore-occupying guests, which was later ascribed to shear mode softening in the case of ZIF-8.[40] These materials were termed $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ generation MOFs, given their inability to remain porous upon pore evacuation.[41] The decomposition of MOFs upon heating, as opposed to melting, however, limits the formation of those glasses in a conventional sense, i.e., formed from the melt.
In an early example, Bennett et al. reported that the thermal decomposition temperature, $T_{\mathrm{d}},$ of a three-dimensional ZIF structure of composition $\mathsf{Z n}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}$ $(\mathrm{Im-C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{2}^{-})$ , could be raised above melting temperature $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ by carefully heating in an inert gas.[21] Curiously, melting in the non-traditional sense was first observed, through collapse and an associated polyamorphic transition into a high-density amorphous (HDA) phase at ca. $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . On further heating, densification to a different crystal (ZIF-Zni) was observed, which subsequently underwent melting at ca. $575~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ As shown in Figure 1 (left panel), the resultant liquid was stable for a further $10~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ above the liquidus temperature, before decomposition. Downwardquenching of the liquid (of identical composition to ZIF-4 and ZIF-zni) engendered a glass state (different from the non-glassy states formed previously) that exhibits a clear glass transition behavior upon reheating, as shown in Figure 1 (right panel), and lacks Bragg peaks in X-ray as shown in Figure 2.
By adjusting the chemistry of the crystalline state, some degree of “tuning” of the melting temperature $(T_{\mathrm{m}})$ of MOFs, alongside the glass-transition temperature $(T_{\mathrm{g}})$ of the derived glasses and the kinetic fragility of the liquids is afforded,[22] which should aid in the expansion of the phenomenon to other MOFs. Despite this family of glasses being termed “melt-quenched MOF glasses”, they, like crystalline MOFs, also fall into the classification of Class II Hybrids, preferred by Drisko and Sanchez,[42] or $\mathrm{I}^{0}\mathrm{O}^{3}$ classification introduced by Cheetham et al.[43]
An important note in the structural characterization of this family is the extension of structural order in their radial distribution functions (RDFs) to almost $1\mathrm{nm}$ , far in excess of other glasses such as silica (6 Å).[44] Such ordering is, however, logical given the similarity of the structure of ZIFs with inorganic zeolites, which is based upon their analogous $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{2})_{4}$ and $\mathrm{SiO}_{4}$ tetrahedral primary building units, as shown in Figure 3.
Compared to flexible bridging oxygen atoms, the much longer length and greater rigidity of imidazolate linkers explain why this new type of glass displays such long-range ordering, considering the fact that the disorder in glasses should develop beyond inter-tetrahedral distances through the bridging angles. Total scattering studies on the glass formed by melting ZIF-4 revealed a near-identical pair distribution function to the HDA phase.[21] Interestingly, simultaneous Reverse Monte Carlo modeling of both synchrotron and neutron data for the HDA phase were used to propose a continuous random network model of tetrahedral $Z\mathrm{n}$ centers connected via imidazolate linkages, analogous to that of amorphous- $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ .[45]
It is not only zeolitic MOFs that can be vitrified by quenching their molten states, but also coordination polymers with lower dimensionalities, e.g., $\mathrm{Zn(H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\mathrm{)}_{2}\mathrm{(HTr)}_{2}$ (Tr: 1,2,4-triazolate, $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{3}^{-})$ and $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}]$ · $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Im}$ .[23–25] In some cases, the dimensionality of the crystalline phase is retained upon glass formation. For example, the one-dimensional (1-D) chain structures of crystalline $\mathrm{[Zn(HPO_{4})(H_{2}P O_{4})_{2}]\cdot2H_{2}I m}$ , and the resultant glass, were confirmed by nuclear magnetic resonance and pair distribution function data.[23,25] Such glasses may be of interest for their characteristics of macroscopic entropic elasticity.[46] Conversely, the related structure $\mathrm{Zn(H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{HTr})_{2}$ adopts an extended two-dimensional connectivity in the crystalline phase, courtesy of repeating $Z\mathrm{n-H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}\mathrm{-}Z\mathrm{n}$ linkages. This connectivity is destroyed upon vitrification, resulting in a zero-dimensional molecular glass.[24] Table 1 lists the melting points $(T_{\mathrm{m}})$ of various MOFs and the glasstransition temperatures $(T_{\mathrm{g}})$ of the glasses obtained by melting them. $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ is defined as the offset temperature of calorimetric melting peak (Figure 1), whereas $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ is the onset temperature of calorimetric glass transition peak. Table 1 demonstrates that both $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ are sensitive to chemical variation in crystalline MOF structure. As a separate consideration, MOFs with identical chemical compositions, which adopt different structures (i.e., polymorphs), have slightly different $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $T_{\mathrm{g}}$ values (Table 1).
The mechanism of melting is of great interest, and in particular the relation to Lindemann’s Law,[47,48] the conventional melting rule, where melting starts to occur when the amplitude of atomic vibration exceeds a critical fraction (about $9\%$ ) of the distance between an atom and its nearest neighbors. Supporting evidence in hybrids comes from the triggering of melting in the $[\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Im}$ coordination polymer by the dissociation of one of the four bonds in the coordination sphere of zinc.[23] Applied to ZIFs, the distances of interest should be the $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ distances, given the relatively weak $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ bonds.[49] It has been suggested that network topology may play a vital role in determining $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ in MOF systems,[21] although it is possible that the topological melting and the Lindemann melting may be linked due to the different bond strengths of identical species in distinct conformations (i.e., $Z\mathrm n^{2+}.$ –ligand bonding in different ZIF polymorphs). It is also likely that the flow units in MOF melts are much larger than those in oxide and metallic melts. This is indirectly confirmed by RDF results,[21,44] since the medium-range-order structure units retained in the former melts are larger than those in the latter ones. Thus, it is expected that the resistance of MOFs to flow at $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ should be larger than that of oxide or metallic systems because the free space for larger flow units is more limited compared to that for smaller ones. In other words, the melting of MOFs is determined by not only the atomic degree of freedom but also by the conformational degree of flow units, although the extent to which the mechanism differs between MOFs and inorganic systems requires further investigation.
The mechanical properties (such as elastic modulus and hardness) and density for melt-quenched MOF glasses are also found to be sensitive to their chemistry. As reported in Ref. [22], vitrification can lead to significant stiffening in MOFs, e.g., in TIF-4, which is reflected in an increase in both elastic modulus and pycnometric density. It has also been found that the elastic moduli of the MOF glasses positively correlate with their pycnometric densities.[22] The hardness values of MOF glasses were observed to lie in the range of $0.2\mathrm{-}1.5~\mathrm{GPa}$ , similar to the previously determined range for crystalline MOFs.
## 3. Potential Technical Applications
## 3.1. Ion Conduction
$\mathrm{H^{+}}.$ -, $\mathrm{Li^{+}}.$ , and $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}.$ -conducting crystalline MOFs have been intensively investigated for use in fuel cell and battery technologies.[50–52] While some researchers have focused on occluding conductive species such as imidazole within MOFs,[53] others concentrate on inherent proton conductivity by frameworks built from, for example, triazole[54] ligands.
A significant drawback of such materials is, however, the limitation on conduction placed by grain boundaries.[23,55] An important advantage of the melt-quenching and recrystallizing process in this respect lies in outstanding shaping abilities, which enables a reduction in the number of grain boundaries. For example, the coordination polymer $([\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Im})$ , which consists of phosphates that bridge the metal ions to form extended 1D chainlike structures, can be fabricated into a thin film by meltquenching and recrystallizing, demonstrating a resistivity of 480 times lower than pellets fabricated by pressing the microcrystalline powder.[23]
## 3.2. Radioactive Waste Storage
Porous materials such as zeolites have long been proposed for use in nuclear waste reprocessing, in separations (e.g., $^{90}\mathrm{Sr}$ and $^{90}\mathrm{Y})$ ),[56] or for selective uptake and subsequent (intermediate) storage of, e.g., iodine.[57] Crystalline MOFs such as ZIF-8 have also been shown to be potentially useful for the latter application, as they possess a greater ability to be chemically tuned for specific target molecules. A secondary encapsulation step in a glass composite was then, however, required in order to facilitate a long-term storage solution.[58] Interestingly, the use of pressure to induce collapse of a crystalline $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ -containing MOF into an amorphous, non-glassy state was demonstrated, with an associated inhibition of guest molecule release, or leeching.[59] The post $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ -capture melting and intrinsic glass formation of a MOF would eliminate the need for a second step, while also perhaps providing a more durable long-term solution—provided guest release upon glass formation can be prevented. This work opens up the possibility of molecular capture and interim storage applications, including volatile fission gases from nuclear fuel reproducing, such as $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and $\operatorname{Kr},$ and other species relevant to clean energy production (e.g., $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\mathbf{\Gamma}}$ ).
## 3.3. Biomedical-Based Applications
While the relatively high temperature of melting precludes the inclusion of antibodies or proteins within MOFs, organic drug molecules may be occluded within MOF “protecting scaffolds”. Compared with the use of other organic (e.g., liposomes and micelles) and inorganic (e.g., zeolites and mesoporous silicas) drug delivery systems, the principal advantages of MOFs lie in their high loading capacities and the possibility of chemically functionalizing the materials to enhance drug affinity and target cells.[60] Here, relatively poor chemical stabilities are considered advantageous because, unlike mesoporous silica, for example, some MOFs are biodegradable. A major limitation is, however, the fast kinetic delivery of the drug due to rapid diffusion from the porous system. Fairen-Jimenez and co-workers[61] demonstrated the trapping of the hydrophilic model molecule calcein $(\mathrm{C}_{46}\mathrm{H}_{46}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{23})$ in the UiO-66 system $\left([\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{6}]\right.$ ( $\mathrm{BDC}=1, $ -benzenedicarboxylate)) through loading into the crystalline system and subsequent ball-milling to induce collapse of the porous system around the drug molecule. Controlled release was significantly prolonged from 2 days to up to 30 days—the latter limit dictated by material degradation. While the exact nature of the MOF–drug complex was not thoroughly investigated, later work by Bennett et. al. showed that free-carboxylate functional groups and defects are generated in UiO-66 upon ball-milling induced amorphization,[62] and hence it is possible that chemisorption, rather than occlusion of the large drug molecule within macropores, lies behind the extended delivery times in this case. Similar to the suggested uses of MOF-glasses for long term radioactive iodine storage, in vivo release rates of small drug molecules may be prolonged through loading and subsequent vitrification of MOFs.
In a combination of (2) and (3), the use of hybrid glasses as radionuclide carriers for in situ tumor irradiation may be envisaged, along with as contrast enhancement agents.
## 3.4. Other Potential Applications
As mentioned above, the vitrification of MOFs allows us to cast or shape glass melts into different morphologies, e.g., bulk glass, thin films, membranes, and fibers. This provides possibilities for the MOF glasses to be used in a wide range of applications. Upon partial or full recrystallization, the pore size distribution and pore structure of MOF glasses can be precisely tailored for controlled gas storage and separation. Furthermore, it is expected that optical and photonic functionalities can be created in MOF glasses, e.g., by tuning their main composition and by introducing rare earth metals. Such materials may be used for fabricating devices with various functions, e.g., luminescence, nonlinear optics, and sensing.
## 4. Outlook and Perspectives
Aside from the possibilities in advancing our fundamental understanding of the glass state, an immense number of potential hybrid glasses may be formed by tuning the chemistry of the crystalline MOF prior to melting. Courtesy of interchangeable organic and inorganic components, one might envisage an array of “designed” glass materials with desired optical, electrical, and mechanical properties as presented above. Clarification of the microscopic mechanism of MOF melting may constitute the theoretical basis for designing new types of MOF glass and may provide insights into glass formation and glass transition in general. These objectives will be realized by performing systematic experiments and computer modeling.
While providing another outlet for research into MOFs and still in its infancy, research into melt-quenched hybrid glasses, along with other hybrid materials, promises to provide exciting opportunities for a great number of practical applications in the future.
